Start with [ good Heritage stock.
](https://www.mcmurrayhatchery.com/chicks.html) Do what they did in 1951.
Select two different breeds for different things. You’ve got your Cornish for
breast size and easy plucking. There is a reason they used Cornish breeds back
then and still do now. They have, by far, the largest proportion of breast
meat to any other breed. And their beautiful, tight feathers pluck well. We
sell both [ Dark ](https://www.mcmurrayhatchery.com/dark_cornish.html) and [
White Laced Red Cornish,
](https://www.mcmurrayhatchery.com/white_laced_red_cornish.html) which are
sure to help you throw some rotund gals into the mix. Then, pick a large bird
to pump up your lines. [ New Hampshires,
](https://www.mcmurrayhatchery.com/new_hampshire_reds.html) [ Delawares
](https://www.mcmurrayhatchery.com/delaware.html) , and [ Rhode Island
](https://www.mcmurrayhatchery.com/rhode_island_reds.html) [ Reds
](https://www.mcmurrayhatchery.com/rhode_island_reds.html) all have really
nice rooster size. Now, let those two breeds dance to create some hybrid
vigor, and you’re on your way to raising your own sustainable meat flock.

